# =============================================================================
# ui/.env.example
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Value-free template of every environment variable the CardDemo single-page
#   application reads. Copy it to ui/.env, fill in the values for whichever
#   environment you are pointing at, and keep that copy local: the copy is
#   untracked, this template is tracked, and that asymmetry is the point of the
#   file.
#
#   Each entry below records the three things a bare name cannot -- what
#   consumes the variable, where a real value originates, and what goes wrong
#   when it is absent. No entry carries a value.
#
# WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
#   - Assumption: the VITE_ prefix is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. Vite
#     exposes ONLY variables matching its `envPrefix` to client code through
#     `import.meta.env` and withholds everything else from the bundle, so the
#     prefix is the mechanical boundary between configuration that may ship to a
#     browser and values that must never leave the server. That is why every
#     name here carries it, and why ui/vite.config.ts must set `envPrefix` to
#     the same string. A name added here WITHOUT the prefix would not fail
#     loudly -- it would simply be invisible at run time.
#   - Assumption: whatever is supplied for these names is compiled INTO the
#     public JavaScript bundle and is readable by anyone who loads the page.
#     They therefore suit non-secret, publicly discoverable configuration only.
#     This is why no credential can legitimately appear here even in a
#     developer's own ui/.env: the browser obtains its bearer token from Cognito
#     at run time, and no password, client secret or access key belongs in this
#     file or in any copy of it, in any form.
#   - Trade-off: every right-hand side is EMPTY, and in particular there is no
#     localhost default. A committed default would appear to BE set, so an
#     environment that never supplied the real value would fail later and
#     further away -- as a connection error against a host that does not exist
#     there -- instead of failing at once as absent configuration. The cost
#     accepted is that a new developer must populate ui/.env before the SPA can
#     reach an API: a one-time, self-evident step, traded for a misconfiguration
#     that cannot hide.
#   - Assumption: none of these values is authored by hand. They originate as
#     Terraform outputs recorded in SSM Parameter Store and Secrets Manager at
#     apply time and are read from there per environment, so no endpoint is
#     hard-coded in the SPA or in a service and no secret reaches a tfvars file.
#     That provenance is what makes "no secrets in the repository" a structural
#     property of the deployment rather than a habit of its reviewers.
#   - Alternatives Considered: a VITE_ENV or VITE_STAGE name was evaluated and
#     rejected. Vite already exposes the build mode as `import.meta.env.MODE`,
#     with the `DEV` and `PROD` booleans derived from it, so a hand-maintained
#     copy would be a second source of truth able to disagree with the mode the
#     bundle was actually built in -- and that disagreement surfaces as
#     behaviour contradicting the build, which is harder to diagnose than having
#     no such variable at all.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API access
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Prefixed onto every request by the shared axios instance in
# ui/src/api/client.ts. It addresses the API Gateway HTTP API that fronts the
# internal load balancer, which is the only way in: the SPA never reaches a
# service or a database directly.
# Assumption: supplied per environment from the API Gateway endpoint Terraform
# emits as an output. Left empty per the header trade-off -- and note what an
# unset value actually does: axios then resolves every call against the origin
# serving the SPA, which answers with the SPA's own HTML instead of JSON, so the
# failure reads as a response-parsing bug rather than as missing configuration.
# That misdirection is precisely why this is never defaulted.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cognito replaces the mainframe USRSEC sign-on file. The four names in this
# section identify the user pool that issues tokens and the region it lives in;
# ui/src/hooks/useAuth.ts holds the resulting token, the group list decoded from
# it, and sign-out.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: privilege in the migrated application comes from a
# SIGNED `cognito:groups` claim -- `carddemo-admin` or `carddemo-user` -- and
# that is the most consequential security correction in the online migration.
# The baseline carried privilege in `CDEMO-USER-TYPE PIC X(01)`, with
# `88 CDEMO-USRTYP-ADMIN VALUE 'A'` and `88 CDEMO-USRTYP-USER VALUE 'U'`
# (app/cpy/COCOM01Y.cpy L26-L28), inside the CICS communication area the
# terminal echoed back between pseudo-conversational turns -- storage the client
# itself returned, so a client could in principle assert its own privilege
# level. A signed claim cannot be asserted by the client. Consequently NO
# variable in this file configures a user type, a group or an administrative
# flag: admin gating reads the claim and nothing else, and introducing such a
# name here would reintroduce exactly the weakness this design removes.
#
# Assumption: a browser SPA is a PUBLIC OAuth client, so the absence of a
# client-secret variable below is deliberate and adding one would be a design
# error rather than a gap in this documentation. Anything in the bundle is
# readable, so a confidential client cannot exist here at all; if a sign-in flow
# appears to need a secret, the pool's app client has been created as
# confidential and the correction belongs in the Cognito Terraform module.

# Issuer base URL of the Cognito user pool, from which the OIDC client derives
# the pool's discovery document in order to validate the tokens it is handed.
# Unset, that client has no discovery endpoint, so it cannot retrieve the pool's
# signing keys and rejects every token as unverifiable.
# Alternatives Considered: naming this VITE_COGNITO_ISSUER. For a user pool the
# two hold the same string, but `authority` is the term OIDC client libraries
# use for the value they are CONFIGURED with, whereas `issuer` names the `iss`
# claim they compare a received token against -- so AUTHORITY describes the job
# this variable does and ISSUER would describe a different one.
# Assumption: emitted by Terraform from the user pool it creates.
VITE_COGNITO_AUTHORITY=

# Identifies the pool itself, for the administrative and sign-out calls that
# take a pool identifier rather than the issuer URL. Unset, a global sign-out
# has no target, so the session ends in the browser while remaining valid at the
# pool -- a divergence that surfaces as no error at all.
# Assumption: a Terraform output. Withheld here because a real pool identifier
# encodes the region and the deployment it belongs to, which is a disclosure
# even though the identifier is not itself a credential.
VITE_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=

# The app client the SPA presents when it begins a sign-in flow; unset, no flow
# can start, because the pool cannot tell which client is asking.
# Assumption: a Terraform output, and public by design -- it identifies the
# client without authenticating it, which is exactly why that app client must be
# configured with no secret (see above).
VITE_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=

# Region hosting the user pool and the API, required by the identity calls that
# are region-scoped rather than URL-addressed.
# Assumption: a Terraform output naming the single region this migration
# targets. Left empty rather than defaulted so that repointing the SPA at
# another environment cannot silently retain a stale region -- a wrong region
# fails as an unresolved pool, which looks like a broken pool rather than a
# broken setting.
VITE_AWS_REGION=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cross-cutting request behaviour
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Names the HTTP header the axios interceptor stamps with a per-request
# correlation identifier, so one action in the browser can be followed across
# every service it fans out to.
# Assumption: the value is dictated by the services rather than chosen here --
# it must match the header the server-side CorrelationIdFilter reads. A mismatch
# fails SILENTLY: no request errors and nothing reports anything wrong; the
# identifier is simply absent from the server's logging context and traces
# quietly stop joining up, which is the worst failure mode available because
# nothing signals it. Keeping the name configurable lets both halves of that
# contract move together, instead of requiring the SPA to be rebuilt to follow a
# server-side rename.
VITE_CORRELATION_ID_HEADER=

# Milliseconds after which the axios instance abandons a request, mirroring the
# explicit connect and read timeouts the services apply to their own synchronous
# calls so that a stalled dependency surfaces as a bounded error rather than a
# screen that hangs indefinitely.
# Assumption: this is an operational tuning decision taken per environment,
# not a provisioned coordinate, so it is the one name here that no Terraform
# output supplies -- which is also what makes the trade-off below a real choice.
# Trade-off: this is the one entry that could defensibly carry a value, since a
# timeout is a behavioural default and discloses nothing environment-specific in
# the way a hostname or an identifier does. It is left empty anyway, to keep the
# file's rule absolute: one populated entry invites the next, and a single grep
# asserting that NO right-hand side is populated is a stronger guard than one
# carrying a documented exception a reader has to evaluate. The cost is that
# ui/src/api/client.ts must supply its own fallback, which is where a
# behavioural default belongs in any case.
VITE_API_TIMEOUT_MS=


# =============================================================================
# Usage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   cp ui/.env.example ui/.env
#
# Assumption: the root .gitignore ignores `.env`, `.env.local` and
# `.env.*.local` while negating `!.env.example` and `!**/.env.example`, so the
# working copy stays untracked while this template stays tracked. That asymmetry
# is deliberate: documenting the configuration contract is a deliverable of the
# migration, and the filled-in copy never is.
# =============================================================================
